WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.570
INTRO:
Semiconductor profit margins have become obscene.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.120
Intel has a gross margin of 50%.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.349
AMD has a gross margin of 48%.

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:13.679
Applied Materials has a gross margin of 47%.

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:16.410
Qualcomm has a gross margin of 58%.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:20.020
And Nvidia has a gross margin of over 60%.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:25.390
Also, keep in mind that that’s just the
average across these entire companies.

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:30.730
So, if we look at the profit margins of their
highest end products, it’s likely even higher

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:31.880
than that.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.160
Take Nvidia for example.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:39.300
Their highest end consumer product, the RTX
4090 was just announced a few weeks ago and

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.580
it starts at an eye watering $1,600.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:50.870
Even if we use Nvidia’s average gross margin
of 60%, this means that $960 is simply gross

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:51.870
profit.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:55.800
But, if we’re being real here, it’s not
like all of Nvidia’s products have a gross

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.350
margin of exactly 60%.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:02.940
The more likely scenario is that the lower
end cards like the 50 and 60 series have a

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:07.650
gross margin of 40 to 50% while the higher
end cards like the 80 and 90 series have a

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.240
gross margin of 70 to 80%.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.610
Even if we estimate on the lower end of this
and call it 70%, this means that it only costs

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:24.190
Nvidia $480 to make the RTX 4090 and they
sell it for $1600 leaving them with $1,120

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:25.520
worth of gross profit.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.230
We should also mention that it’s not just
these front facing companies that have obscene

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.090
margins either.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.340
Backend semiconductor companies are also living
it up.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:41.740
ASML gross margins stand at 51% while TSMC’s
gross margins stand at 55%.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:46.030
Considering this, it’s unlikely that producing
the 4090 costs even $200.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:49.540
Yet, it’s being sold for 8 times that amount.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:54.090
For perspective, even the iPhone which is
likely the most criticized product in the

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:59.780
entire world for having high margins is estimated
to cost $570 to make.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:04.990
This means that even the iPhone is only being
sold for 2 times its production cost while

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.210
chips are being sold for several times their
production cost.

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:13.330
Now, you might be saying that we should look
at net margin instead of gross margin, but

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.980
this doesn’t really change all that much.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.360
ASML has a net margin of 30%.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.310
TSMC has a net margin of 40%.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.709
Qualcomm has a net margin of 30%.

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:27.129
And Nvidia has a net margin of 26%.

00:02:27.129 --> 00:02:32.219
This means that even if you include all of
the expenses of research, development, marketing,

00:02:32.219 --> 00:02:36.360
and everything else that it takes to run these
companies, both the suppliers and the front

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.180
facing companies are left with 30% margins
each.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.550
If you’re a shareholder of one of these
companies, you’re holding pure gold.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:49.249
I mean Nvidia stock is up 45X in the past
10 years even with the recent 63% sell off.

00:02:49.249 --> 00:02:55.540
But, if you’re a customer of one of these
companies, well it’s not as great.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.860
Something that makes this even more suprirsing
is that chips are not the new kid on the block

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:00.860
either.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:05.599
Most of these companies have been around for
30 to 50 years, so you would think that their

00:03:05.599 --> 00:03:09.790
margins would’ve worn off with time as new
competitors entered the market.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:11.569
Yet, here we are.

00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:16.549
So, how in the world did semiconductor margins
become so obscene.

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:22.139
FUNDAMENTAL RISK:
Taking a look back, semiconductor margins

00:03:22.139 --> 00:03:27.659
have always been on the higher side of things,
and the main reason for this is the substantial

00:03:27.659 --> 00:03:29.889
risk that’s involved in running a chip company.

00:03:29.889 --> 00:03:35.669
You see, the chip industry is one of the few
industries in which you always have to perform

00:03:35.669 --> 00:03:38.120
simply because its so ruthless.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.900
And the main reason for this is something
called Moore’s law.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:47.319
This law was created way back in the 1960s
by one of the cofounders of Intel: Gordon

00:03:47.319 --> 00:03:48.319
Moore.

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:52.420
Based on the historical progression of semiconductors,
Gordon projected that the number of transistors

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:57.409
in a dense integrated circuit would double
every year for the next 10 years.

00:03:57.409 --> 00:04:01.120
After these ten years, he would revise his
theory to say that the number of transistors

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.829
would continue to double every two years indefinitely.

00:04:04.829 --> 00:04:09.140
This observation has since become the standard
within the chip industry and has turned out

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.370
to be frighteningly accurate.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:16.019
Over the years, there has been so many sceptics
and even experts suggesting that Moore’s

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:17.110
law is dead.

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:21.380
But, you just have to take one look at this
logarithmic graph to know that Moore’s law

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:23.610
is still very much alive.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:27.850
The number of transistors on microchips has
literally grown from a few thousand in the

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:30.889
1970s to tens of billions today.

00:04:30.889 --> 00:04:36.800
Honestly, this growth is extraordinary and
is simply a testament to just how much humans

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:37.800
can accomplish.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:42.110
But, wait a minute, how is this relevant to
high chip margins?

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:47.259
Well, the answer is that it’s extremely
risky to be in the chip business because you

00:04:47.259 --> 00:04:50.180
constantly have to innovate at record pace.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.830
If you don’t, you’ll be dead within a
few years.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:57.510
Here’s a list of all of the prominent American
chip companies that have gone bankrupt within

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:58.510
the past few decades.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:04.020
I don’t think there’s any industry that
his been so cutthroat for so long.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.840
Take the iPhone for example which has only
been around for 15 years.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:14.599
I don’t know about you but the iPhone 14
definitely does not seem to be a 2X upgrade

00:05:14.599 --> 00:05:16.650
from the iPhone 12.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:21.470
Not only do you have to innovate like no other
industry, but you also have an extremely small

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:22.800
margin for error.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:28.080
Here’s the thing, chip companies are solely
dependent on revenue from their chips.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.150
If iPhone sales go down, Apple can pivot to
services.

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:36.880
If Amazon sales slow down, Amazon can raise
the price of Prime.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:42.270
But, if chip sales go down, chip companies
have nothing to pivot to.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:47.030
Most of them have no contact with the end
user and no forms of recurring revenue.

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:52.360
The only way they make money is by selling
chips and the only way the sell chips is by

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.389
leading the innovation.

00:05:54.389 --> 00:05:58.539
Their corporate customers have very little
brand loyalty and are willing to switch at

00:05:58.539 --> 00:05:59.539
anytime.

00:05:59.539 --> 00:06:02.790
I mean, Apple literally ditched Intel.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:06.849
Do you really think they’re gonna have a
problem with ditching someone like Qualcomm?

00:06:06.849 --> 00:06:09.060
The answer is no.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:13.870
And if none of that convinced you that this
business is risky, just take a look at Intel

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:17.540
stock which hasn’t made a new all time high
in 22 years.

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:22.909
So, while the semiconductor industry can be
extremely rewarding, it can also be brutally

00:06:22.909 --> 00:06:24.159
painful.

00:06:24.159 --> 00:06:28.719
And one of the main reasons these companies
have such high margins is to compensate for

00:06:28.719 --> 00:06:30.639
that risk.

00:06:30.639 --> 00:06:36.449
UNPRECEDENTED DEMAND:
While chip companies have always boasted solid

00:06:36.449 --> 00:06:40.750
margins when they’re on top, the margins
that we’re seeing today are higher than

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:43.370
anything we’ve ever seen before.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:44.860
Take Nvidia for example.

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:51.330
Before 2016, the company had a much more modest
and reasonable net margin of 10 to 15%.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:56.449
But, between 2016 and 2019, these margins
skyrocketed to 38%.

00:06:56.449 --> 00:07:00.479
A similar trend can be seen at AMD as well.

00:07:00.479 --> 00:07:04.900
Up until 2016, AMD was actually consistently
losing money.

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:11.990
But, between 2016 and 2019, AMD’s margins
flipped from -20% to positive 5%.

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:17.139
TSMC enjoyed a similar boost as well just
during a slightly different time frame.

00:07:17.139 --> 00:07:22.749
So, what happened in the mid 2010s that caused
semiconductor margins to rocket across the

00:07:22.749 --> 00:07:23.749
board?

00:07:23.749 --> 00:07:27.470
Well, the answer is unprecedented spikes in
demand.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:31.409
Within the broader market, the source of this
spike is obvious.

00:07:31.409 --> 00:07:33.669
It was the smartphone revolution.

00:07:33.669 --> 00:07:39.819
Between 2009 and 2018, the number of smartphones
sold per year rocketed from 172 million units

00:07:39.819 --> 00:07:42.870
to 1.5 billion units.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:45.749
Many companies saw this opportunity before
it happened.

00:07:45.749 --> 00:07:50.909
TSMC’s founder, Morris Chang, literally
came out of retirement just to make sure TSMC

00:07:50.909 --> 00:07:54.090
didn’t screw up this gigantic oppurtunity.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:58.590
Under Chang’s guidance, TSMC would eventually
score a deal with Apple and the company would

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:00.590
proceed to go vertical shortly after.

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:05.180
The smartphone boom wasn’t the only surge
in chip demand either.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:09.270
We saw another boom within the PC gaming space
thanks to AMD.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:14.409
With the launch of their Ryzen line of CPUs
in 2017, AMD made PC gaming more accessible

00:08:14.409 --> 00:08:18.979
than ever before by bringing down the cost
of building a gaming PC.

00:08:18.979 --> 00:08:24.210
Shortly after the launch of Ryzen, you could
build a solid gaming PC for well under a $1000.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:28.789
And when you combine this with the insane
popularity of Fortnite, you had a bunch of

00:08:28.789 --> 00:08:31.919
casual mobile and console gamers switch to
PC.

00:08:31.919 --> 00:08:36.750
But, unfortunately, this honeymoon period
didn’t last that long because gamers weren’t

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.540
the only ones that were trying to secure GPUs.

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:44.680
You see, around the same time that PC gaming
was blowing up, so were cryptocurrencies.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:49.640
Between 2015 and 2017, Bitcoin would literally
100x from $200 to $20,000.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:56.710
Bitcoin dragged along the entire crypto market
along with it which made it extremely profitable

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:59.550
to mine ethereum using Nvidia GPUs.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:04.110
And these crypto miners weren’t just buying
1 or 2 gpus at a time.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:09.700
They were buying dozens because this wasn’t
just entertainment to them, this was a business.

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:14.290
Soon enough, Nvidia wouldn’t be able to
keep up with demand which led to gpus selling

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:18.800
on the secondary market for insane markups
of 50 to 100%.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:24.140
Despite this, miners kept buying GPUs because
it was still profitable, and this is when

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:27.060
a light bulb went off in Jensen Huang’s
head.

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:31.921
If people are willing to pay 50 to 100% more
for our gpus on the secondary market, why

00:09:31.921 --> 00:09:34.770
not just charge 50 to 100% ourselves?

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:40.420
With the launch of the 2000 series, this is
exactly what Nvidia would do.

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:44.670
Initially, this actually backfired for Nvidia
because the 2000 series came out during the

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:47.130
depths of the 2018 bear market.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:51.730
But, Nvidia kept the high pricing for the
3000 series which put them in the perfect

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:55.180
position for the 2021 crypto bull market and
the rest is history.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:10:01.970
THE END IS NEAR:
Though all of these demand factors were building

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:06.560
up throughout the entire decade, the grand
slam didn’t come till 2020 which was of

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:08.590
course the beginning of the supply shortage.

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:13.010
As the pandemic took hold and factories and
logistics channel around the world began to

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:14.430
shut down.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:18.690
And if you know anything about manufacturing
and scaling, you know that its way easier

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:22.380
to shut down the supply chain than it is to
bring it back to full capacity.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:26.110
And this recovery has simply been worsened
by inflation and recession fears.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:30.180
You see, when manufacturers have to pay insane
amounts for raw materials and when they’re

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:35.540
fearful that demand is going to slow, they’re
going to be hesitant to comes to pumping up

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:36.540
production.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:42.780
Initially, this was a disadvantage as companies
couldn’t meet market demand, but over time,

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:44.760
this has actually become an advantage.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:49.600
When there’s a discrepancy between supply
and demand, companies can further raise prices

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:53.530
and simply blame it on inflation and supply
constraints.

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:58.110
Everyone from milk supplies to Apple has been
doing this, so it’s no surprise that chip

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:01.070
companies have jumped onto the trend as well.

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:05.480
But all of this brings up the question: how
long can this actually last.

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:10.050
Whether it’s next year or 3 years from now,
the supply shortage will eventually come to

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:11.130
an end.

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:14.720
Not to mention, all of the growth factors
that we previously discussed are also slowly

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:16.970
coming to an end as well.

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:21.020
In terms of smartphone adoption, everyone
already has a smartphone.

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:25.730
Global smartphone sales literally peaked 4
years ago and with the differences between

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:30.170
each smartphone iteration becoming less and
less discernable, it’s likely that smartphones

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:32.770
sales will not only stagnate but decline.

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:37.730
So, chip suppliers like TSMC will have less
and less pricing power over customers like

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:38.730
Apple.

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:42.920
At the same time, the crypto community is
moving away from mining as a whole and switching

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:44.200
over proof of stake.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:49.300
Ethereum just switched to proof of stake just
a few weeks ago, and that probably set a precedent

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:50.450
moving forward.

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:55.380
Now, none of this is to say that the chip
industry is going to collapse.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:59.430
Semiconductors have become an essential part
of modern society, so the industry as a whole

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:02.030
is not going anywhere anytime soon.

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:07.850
But, with that being said, the chip industry
is extremely cyclical and it looks like we

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:11.820
may have just completed the boom phase at
the end of last year.

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:16.920
This means that we’re probably just at the
beginning of a long and nasty bust phase.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:22.930
Nvidia had one of these phases after the dotcom
boom and it took Nvidia 15 years to get back

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:24.110
into another boom phase.

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:27.320
As a customer, this is probably a good thing.

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:31.740
As demand levels out and supply issues are
resolved, we’ll probably see significantly

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:32.990
better value per dollar.

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:37.950
If you’re an investor, however, you better
buckle up because regardless of how long this

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:42.130
phase lasts, one things for sure: it’s gonna
be volatile.

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:43.760
But that’s just what I think.

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:46.430
Do you think semiconductor margins are too
high?

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:47.980
Comment that down below.

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:52.450
Also, drop a like if you’re hoping that
the end of this insanity is near.

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:56.220
And of course, consider checking out our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:12:56.220 --> 00:13:02.310
consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

